Explanation:
Step-by-Step Solution:
1. Statutory Text: Section 34, Republic Act No. 8491 (Prohibited Acts regarding the National Flag):
   - 'It shall be prohibited:
     (a) To mutilate, deface, defile, trample on or cast contempt or commit any act or omission casting dishonor or ridicule upon the flag over its surface;
     (b) To dip the flag to any person or object by way of compliment or salute;
     (c) To use the flag:
         (1) As a drapery, festoon, tablecloth;
         (2) As covering for ceilings, walls, statues or other objects;
         (3) As a pennant on the hood, side, back and top of motor vehicles;
         (4) As a staff or whip;
         (5) For unveiling monuments or statues;
         (6) As trademarks, or for industrial, commercial or advertising designs;
     (d) To display the flag:
         (1) Under any painting or picture;
         (2) Horizontally face-up;
         (3) In any place of vice;
     (e) To wear the flag in whole or in part as a costume or uniform;
     (f) To add any word, figure, mark, picture, design, drawings, advertisement, or imprint of any nature on the flag.'
2. Mandatory Disposal of Worn Flags (Section 14): A flag that has become worn, torn, or faded shall NOT be thrown away or used as cleaning rags; it MUST BE SOLEMNLY BURNED to avoid desecration.
3. Option A is the verbatim enumeration of prohibited acts.

Common Trap: Thinking it is patriotic to wear a flag as a shirt or superhero cape; wearing the flag as clothing or costume is a CRIMINAL PROHIBITED ACT under RA 8491.
Exam Tip: Flag Prohibitions (RA 8491, Sec. 34): Cannot be used as tablecloth/drapery, cannot be worn as costume/clothing, cannot be printed on commercial ads, cannot add trademarks. Frayed flags must be SOLEMNLY BURNED.
